{% comment %}
  blocks/_expert-quote-feature.liquid

  The picture directly under the expert, with the offset outline behind it.

  ## Why it is its own block and not part of the lead

  It is a different kind of thing. The lead is a claim — words and the person
  who made them — and this is a product. A merchant swapping which product the
  endorsement points at should not be opening the block that holds the quote,
  and a merchant rewriting the quote should not be scrolling past image
  settings to reach it.

  `sections/expert-quote.liquid` renders it with `content_for 'block'`, so it is
  editable but cannot be dragged out of the centre column or deleted by
  accident — the same treatment the lead gets, and the same mechanism
  `sections/testimonials.liquid` uses for its intro panel.

  ## The frame behind it

  A second outline, the picture's own size, nudged off it — the detail that
  stops the centre column reading as a plain stack. It is a pseudo-element on
  the same box translated by two offsets, so it is always exactly the picture's
  size and follows the corner radius without either being restated. At an offset
  of 0/0 it becomes a plain outline around the picture, which is a reasonable
  thing for a merchant to want.

  ## It is never an empty box

  With no upload, `snippets/image.liquid` draws a placeholder. A merchant
  arranging this section should be looking at the shape of the picture while
  they decide which one it is, not at a gap they have to imagine around.

  ## The loop is the same vocabulary the scattered pictures use

  Four styles, one set of keyframes in `assets/base.css`, shared by this block
  and `blocks/_expert-quote-image.liquid`. The distance and the speed come from
  the section so all the pictures move to one rhythm; the style and the offset
  into the cycle are per-picture, because pictures rising and falling in unison
  is a lift rather than a drift.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign b = block.settings

  assign width = b.width | default: 232 | plus: 0
  assign width_mobile = b.width_mobile | default: width | plus: 0
  assign radius = b.radius | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign radius_mobile = b.radius_mobile | default: radius | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the outline ----

    Its own style, colour and offsets rather than the section's frame settings,
    because the two are different marks: the frame is around the whole section
    and this is a deliberate off-register echo of one photograph. A merchant
    turning the section's frame off should not lose this.

    Drawn only when it has both a style and a width — an outline 0px wide with a
    colour on it is three declarations that paint nothing. Opacity goes into the
    colour rather than onto the element, so the picture is not faded along with
    its outline; `currentColor` cannot take an alpha channel from the colour
    filters, so `color-mix` does the same fade in the browser instead.
  endcomment
  assign outline_style = b.outline_style | default: 'none'
  assign outline_width = b.outline_width | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign has_outline = false
  if outline_style != 'none' and outline_width > 0
    assign has_outline = true
  endif

  if has_outline
    assign outline_percent = b.outline_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if b.outline_color == blank
      assign outline_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: outline_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign outline_alpha = outline_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign outline_paint = b.outline_color | color_modify: 'alpha', outline_alpha
    endif
  endif

  comment
    ---- the loop ----

    The style is a class so one keyframe set in the stylesheet serves every
    picture in the section; the offset is a custom property, written negative so
    the picture starts that far into the cycle rather than standing still for
    that long first.
  endcomment
  assign motion = b.motion_style | default: 'none'

  comment
    The picture is never wider than the column it sits in, so the `sizes` hint
    is the slider rather than a viewport fraction. The browser is told the one
    number that is actually true about the box, and stops fetching a 1600px
    variant for a 232px frame.
  endcomment
  assign sizes = width_mobile | append: 'px'
  assign sizes = '(min-width: 750px) ' | append: width | append: 'px, ' | append: sizes

  assign classes = 'expert-quote-pic expert-quote-pic--feature'
  if motion != 'none'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' expert-quote-pic--' | append: motion
  endif
  if has_outline
    assign classes = classes | append: ' expert-quote-pic--outlined'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture block_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: b, declarations: true -%}
  --eqp-width: {{ width }}px;
  --eqp-width-mobile: {{ width_mobile }}px;
  --eqp-radius: {{ radius }}px;
  --eqp-radius-mobile: {{ radius_mobile }}px;
  --eqp-space: {{ b.space | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    The featured picture is not tilted, but the keyframes are shared with the
    scattered ones that are — and the sway style rotates *around* whatever tilt
    a picture already carries. Publishing 0 here rather than leaving it unset
    means one set of keyframes works for both without a second copy that
    differs only in its resting angle.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --eqp-rotate: 0deg;
  --eqp-motion-delay: -{{ b.motion_delay | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}ms;
  {%- if has_outline -%}
    --eqp-outline-width: {{ outline_width }}px;
    --eqp-outline-style: {{ outline_style }};
    --eqp-outline-color: {{ outline_paint }};
    --eqp-outline-x: {{ b.outline_offset_x | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
    --eqp-outline-y: {{ b.outline_offset_y | default: 0 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<motion-effect
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ block_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'image', block: b, target: '.expert-quote-pic__frame' %}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="expert-quote-pic__frame">
    {% render 'image',
       image: b.image,
       ratio: b.ratio,
       sizes: sizes,
       placeholder: 'hero-apparel-1',
       wrapper_class: 'expert-quote-pic__media' %}
  </div>
</motion-effect>

